  ],
  "hints": [
    "sorting after combining ignores that both lists were already sorted. what does that fact let you skip?",
    "if you're looking at the front of both lists, which one definitely holds the next-smallest overall value?",
    "keep a pointer into each list; repeatedly take whichever pointer's current value is smaller, append it to the result, and advance that pointer",
    "once one list runs out, the rest of the other list can just be appended as-is, since it's already sorted"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting to append the remaining tail of whichever list still has elements left after the other is exhausted",
    "comparing incorrectly when values are equal (either order is fine, just be consistent)",
    "sorting the merged result anyway instead of using the two-pointer merge, missing the point of using the pre-sorted inputs"
  ],
  "content": {
